== Chapter XIII

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Exhorting to the heavenly calling.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

2\. #smallcaps[Labor] to make it clear to your#super[+] own souls that you are savingly called (2 Pet 1:10): “Strive to make your calling … sure.” This is the great business of our lives: to get sound evidence of our #emph[effectual calling.] Do not rest content with outward privileges. Do not cry, as the Jews did, “The temple of the #smallcaps[Lord].” Do not rest in baptism; what is it to have the water and lack the Spirit? Do not be content that you were born within the sound of #emph[Aaron’s] bells, that Christ has been preached to you. Do not satisfy yourselves with an empty profession. All this may be so, and yet you may be no better than blazing comets. But labor to make it evident to your souls that you are #emph[called by God.] Do not be #emph[Athenians,] asking for news: What is the state and complexion of the times? What will be the effects of such an eclipse? What changes are likely to happen in such a year? What is all this if you are not #emph[effectually called?] What if the times should take on a fairer look? What if #emph[glory dwelt in our land,] if grace does not dwell in our hearts?#footnote[Ps 85:9.] O my brothers, when things are dark outside, let all be clear within. “Strive to make your calling … sure.” It is both possible and likely; God does not fail those who seek him. Do not let this great business hang unfinished any longer. If there were a dispute about your land, you would use every means to clear your title. And is salvation nothing? Will you not clear your title here? Consider how sad your case is if you are not #emph[effectually called.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. You#super[+] are strangers to God. The prodigal went into a far country (Luke 15:13), to show that every sinner before conversion is #emph[far off] from God (Eph 2:12): “At that time you#super[+] were separate from Christ, … strangers to the covenants of the promise.” Men dying in their sins have no more right to the promises than strangers have to the privileges of freeborn citizens. If you are strangers, what word can you expect from God but this: “I do not know you”?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. If you#super[+] are not #emph[effectually called,] you are enemies (Col 1:21): “alienated from God and … enemies in your minds.” There is nothing in the Bible you can lay claim to but the threats. You are heirs to all the plagues written in the book of God. Though you may resist the commands of the law, you cannot escape the curses of the law. Let those who are enemies to God read their doom in Luke 19:27: “These enemies of mine who were unwilling for me to rule over them, bring them here and slay them in front of me.” Oh, then, how closely it concerns you to make your #emph[calling] sure! How miserable and damnable your condition will be if death calls you before the Spirit calls you!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Question] 1. But is there any hope of my being called? I have been a great sinner.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Answer.] Great sinners have been called. #emph[Paul] was as bloody a persecutor as #emph[Domitian] or #emph[Julian] ever was, yet he was called#footnote[μήτε ὁ πρὸς τὸν πυθμένα τῆς κακίας κατελθὼν ἀπελπιζέτω ἑαυτοῦ τὴν σωτηρίαν. \[Let not even one who has gone down to the very bottom of wickedness despair of his own salvation.\] #emph[Chrysostom.]]. Some of the Jews who had a hand in crucifying Christ were called. God loves to display the flag of free grace to sinners. So do not be discouraged. You see a golden cord let down from heaven for poor trembling souls to lay hold of.]]

=== Signs of Effectual Calling

#emph[Question] 2. But how shall I know that I am #emph[effectually called?]

#emph[Answer.] 1. He who is #emph[savingly called] is called out of himself, not only out of #emph[sinful self] but out of #emph[righteous self.] He renounces trust in his duties and moral endowments (Phil 3:9): “not having my own righteousness.” He whose heart God has touched by his Spirit lays down the idol of self-righteousness at Christ’s feet for him to tread on. He practices morality and duties of godliness, but he does not trust in them. #emph[Noah’s] dove made use of her wings to fly, but trusted in the ark for safety. It is an excellent thing when a man is called out of himself. This self-renunciation is (as #emph[Augustine] says) #emph[primus ad fidem aditus,] the first step to saving faith.

2\. He who is #emph[effectually called] has a visible change worked in him: not a change of the #emph[faculties,] but of the #emph[qualities.] He is altered from what he was before. His #emph[body] is the same, but not his #emph[mind.] He has #emph[a different spirit]#footnote[Num 14:24.]; he is #emph[alter idem] \[another, and yet the same\]. #emph[Paul] was so changed after his conversion that people did not know him (Acts 9:21). Oh, what a transformation grace makes! 1 Cor 6:11: “And that is what some of you#super[+] were. But you#super[+] were … sanctified, you#super[+] were justified”#footnote[Abluti #emph[a sordibus peccatorum] Sanctificati #emph[per renovationem interioris hominis.] \[Washed from the filth of sins; sanctified by the renewal of the inner man.\] Clarius.]. Grace is the true #emph[Verticordia;] it turns the heart. In #emph[effectual calling] a threefold change is worked.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. A change is worked in the #emph[understanding.] Before, there was ignorance; “darkness was over the surface of the deep.” But #emph[now] there is light (Eph 5:8): “Now you#super[+] are light in the Lord.” The first work #emph[God] made was light, and so it is in the new creation. He who is savingly called says with that man in the gospel (John 9:25), “I was blind, but now I see!” He sees an evil in sin, and an excellence in the ways of God, such as he never saw before. Indeed, this light that the blessed Spirit brings may well be called θαυμαστὸν φῶς, #emph[a marvelous light] (1 Pet 2:9): “to proclaim the virtues of Him who called you#super[+] … into His marvelous light”#footnote[#emph[Inseritur θαυμαστὸν ad denotandam hujus lucis excellentiam.] \[θαυμαστὸν is put in to show the excellence of this light.\] Grotius.]. It is a marvelous light in six respects. (1) Because it is strangely conveyed. It does not come from the heavenly spheres, where the planets are, but from the Sun of righteousness. (2) It is marvelous in its effect. This light does what no other light can: it makes a man see that he is blind. (3) It is a marvelous light because it is more penetrating. Other light may shine on the face; this light shines on the heart and enlightens the conscience (2 Cor 4:6). (4) It is a marvelous light because it sets those who have it marveling. They marvel at themselves, how they could be content so long without it. They marvel that their eyes should be opened and not others’. They marvel that, though they hated and opposed this light, it should still shine in the sky of their souls. This is what the saints will stand wondering at for all eternity. (5) It is a marvelous light because it has more power than any other. It not only enlightens, but quickens; it makes alive those who #emph[were dead in their trespasses and sins.] That is why it is so fittingly called #emph[lumen vitae, the light of life] (John 8:12). (6) It is a marvelous light because it is the beginning of the everlasting light. The light of grace is the morning star that ushers in the sunlight of glory.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now then, Christian, can you say that this marvelous light of the Spirit has dawned on you? When you were wrapped in ignorance and knew neither #emph[God] nor yourself, suddenly #emph[a light from heaven shone around you.] This is one part of the blessed change that is worked in #emph[effectual calling.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. A change is worked in the #emph[will] (Rom 7:18): “I have the will to do what is good.” The will, which before opposed Christ, now embraces him. The will, which was an iron sinew, is now like melting wax; it readily receives the stamp and impression of the Holy Spirit. The will, like the #emph[primum mobile] \[the first mover\], moves heavenward and carries all the spheres of the affections along with it. The regenerate will answers every call of God as the echo answers the voice (Acts 9:6): “What should I do, Lord?” The will now becomes a volunteer; it enlists under the #emph[Captain of salvation]#footnote[Heb 2:10.]. Oh, what a happy change is worked here! Before, the will kept Christ out; now it keeps sin out.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. There is a change in the #emph[conduct.] He who is called by God walks directly contrary to the way he walked before. Before, he walked in envy and malice; now he walks in love. Before, he walked in pride; now in humility. The current is carried quite another way (Acts 23:1): “I have conducted myself before God in all good conscience.” As in the heart there is a #emph[new birth,] so in the life there is a #emph[new edition.] So we see what a mighty change is worked in those who are called by God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[How far from this #emph[effectual call] are those who have never had any change! They are the same as they were forty or fifty years ago, as proud and carnal as ever. They have seen many changes in the times, but they have had no change in their hearts. Let no man think to go to heaven #emph[per saltum,] to leap out of the harlot’s lap into #emph[Abraham’s] bosom. Either they must have a gracious change while they live, or a cursed change when they die.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. He who is called by God values this call as the highest blessing. A king whom #emph[God] has called by his grace counts it more that he is called to be a saint than that he is called to be a king. He values his #emph[high calling] above his #emph[high birth.] #emph[Theodosius] thought it a greater honor to be a #emph[Christian] than to be an #emph[emperor.] A carnal person can no more value spiritual blessings than a child can value a cluster of diamonds. He prefers his worldly grandeur, his ease, his plenty, his titles of honor, to conversion. He would rather be called #emph[Duke] than #emph[Saint;] it is a sign that he is a stranger to effectual calling. He who is enlightened by the Spirit counts holiness his best coat of arms, and looks on his #emph[effectual calling] as his #emph[promotion.] When he has taken this degree, he is a candidate for heaven.]

4\. He who is effectually called is called out of the world. It is a #emph[heavenly calling] (Heb 3:1). He who is called by God sets his mind on things of a heavenly kind. He is in the world, but not #emph[of] the world. Naturalists say of precious stones that though they have their matter from the earth, their sparkling luster comes from the influence of the heavens. So it is with a godly man: though his body is from the earth, the sparkle of his affections is from heaven. His heart is drawn up into the #emph[upper region,] as high as Christ. He casts off not only every #emph[wicked work] but every #emph[earthly weight.] He is not a #emph[worm] but an #emph[eagle.]

5\. Another sign of our #emph[effectual calling] is diligence in our #emph[ordinary calling.] Some boast of their #emph[high calling,] but they lie idle at anchor. Religion does not sign warrants for idleness. Christians must not be like #emph[Leviathan,] which was made to #emph[frolic in the sea] (Ps 104:26). Idleness is #emph[balneum diaboli,] the #emph[devil’s bath;] a lazy person becomes a prey to every temptation. Grace, while it cures the heart, does not make the hand lame. He who is called by God works at his trade, just as he works for heaven#footnote[#emph[Vt rosa de spinis nascitur, ita e laboribus fructus capitur jucundissimus.] \[As the rose springs from thorns, so the sweetest fruit is gathered from labors.\] Erasmus.]. Now if on searching you#super[+] can find that you are effectually called, I have three exhortations for you.
